          %p
            %strong Literature.
            (
            %a{:href => "#Kochman96"}> Kochman 96
            ).
          %h2#S1GeneralizedCohomology Generalized cohomology
          %p
            %strong Idea.
            The concept that makes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            be about methods of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            applied to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topology"} topology
            is that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+homology"} generalized homology
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+functors"} covariant functors
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contravariant+functors"} contravariant functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spaces
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi Ab
                    %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Spaces \longrightarrow Ab^{\mathbb{Z}}
          %p
            from (sufficiently nice)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
            to
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+abelian+groups"} graded abelian groups
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+types"} homotopy types
            of topological spaces is preserved as one passes them from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ho%28Top%29"} Ho(Top)
            to the much more tractable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
          %p
            %strong Literature.
            (
            %a{:href => "#AguilarGitlerPrieto02"}> Aguilar-Gitler-Prieto 02, chapters 7,8 and 12
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kochman96"} Kochman 96, 3.4, 4.2
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Schwede12"} Schwede 12, II.6
          %h3#GeneralizedHomologyAndCohomologyFunctors Generalized cohomology functors
          %p
            %strong Idea.
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theory is such a contravariant functor which satisfies the key properties exhibited by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            (as computed for instance by
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+invariance"} homotopy invariance
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/excision"} excision
            %em except
            that its value on the point is not required to be concentrated in degree 0. Dually for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+homology"} generalized homology
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+cohomology"} reduced cohomology
          %p
            An important example of a generalised cohomology theory other than
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+cohomology"} cobordism cohomology
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/stable+cohomotopy"} stable cohomotopy
          %p
            %strong Literature.
            (
            %a{:href => "#Switzer75"}> Switzer 75, section 7
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AguilarGitlerPrieto02"} Aguilar-Gitler-Prieto 02, section 12 and section 9
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Kochman96"} Kochman 96, 3.4
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %h4#reduced_cohomology Reduced cohomology
          %p The traditional formulation of reduced generalized cohomology in terms of point-set topology is this:
          #ReducedGeneralizedCohomology.num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+cohomology+theory"} reduced cohomology theory
              is
            %ol
              %li
                %p
