          %h3#BrownRepresentabilityTheorem Brown representability theorem
          %p
            %strong Idea.
            Given any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            such as the generalized (co)homology functor
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#GeneralizedHomologyAndCohomologyFunctors"} above
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable functor
            = succeed "-grading" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/suspension+isomorphisms"} suspension isomorphisms
            = succeed "-indexed" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
            such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/reduced+suspension"} reduced suspension
            of one is comparable to the next one in the list. This is a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            or more specifically: a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sequential+spectrum"} sequential spectrum
            \.
          %p
            Whitehead observed that indeed every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectrum
            represents a generalized (co)homology theory. The
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brown+representability+theorem"} Brown representability theorem
            states that, conversely, every generalized (co)homology theory is represented by a spectrum, subject to conditions of additivity.
          %p
            As a first application,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectra"} Eilenberg-MacLane spectra
            representing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ordinary+cohomology"} ordinary cohomology
            may be characterized via Brown representability.
          %p
            %strong Literature.
            (
            %a{:href => "#Switzer75"}> Switzer 75, section 9
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#AguilarGitlerPrieto02"} Aguilar-Gitler-Prieto 02, section 12
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Kochman96"} Kochman 96, 3.4
          %h4#BrownRepresentabilityTraditional Traditional discussion
          %p
            Write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Top
                    %mrow
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo ↪
                  %msup
                    %mi Top
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Top_{{\geq 1}}^{\ast/} \hookrightarrow Top^{\ast/}
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pointed+topological+spaces"} pointed topological spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Set
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set^{\ast/}
            for the category of
