            %h6#proof_17 Proof
            %p
              Via prop.
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CohomologyFunctorOnInfinityCategoryIsBrownFunctor"}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#BrownRepresentabilityOnPresentableInfinityCategories"}
              gives the first clause. With this, the second clause follows by the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
          %h3#S2MilnorExactSequence Milnor exact sequence
          %p
            %strong Idea.
            One tool for computing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+cohomology"} generalized cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+groups"} groups
            via “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+limits"}> inverse limits
            ” are
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Milnor+exact+sequences"} Milnor exact sequences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B U(1)
            plays a key role in the
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+oriented+cohomology"} complex oriented cohomology
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#ComplexOrientedCohomologyTheory"} below
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B U(1) \simeq \mathbb{C}P^\infty
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type"} homotopy type
            of the infinite
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+projective+space"} complex projective space
            (def.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#ComplexProjectiveSpace"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+limit"} direct limit
            of finite dimensional projective spaces
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+limit"} inverse limit
            of the generalized cohomology groups of the
            = succeed "s." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor"} derived functor
            of the
            = succeed "-operation" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
            – and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Milnor+exact+sequence"} Milnor exact sequence
            expresses how the naive limits receive corrections from higher “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lim%5E1"}> lim^1
            \-terms”. In practice one mostly proceeds by verifying conditions under which these corrections happen to disappear, these are the
            = succeed "." do
